<section class="how">
    <h2>How to Use This TDEE Calculator</h2>
    <p>Enter your gender, age, weight in kilograms, and height in centimeters. Then choose the activity level that honestly matches your average week. Most people overestimate how active they are, so when in doubt go one level lower.</p>
    <p>Hit Calculate and the tool gives you three numbers: your TDEE for maintenance, a cutting target for fat loss, and a bulking target for muscle gain. The cutting number is roughly 500 calories below TDEE, which works out to about half a kilogram of fat loss per week. The bulking number adds about 300 to 500 calories above maintenance for slow, lean mass gain.</p>
    <p>Your TDEE is not fixed. As your weight changes, so does your metabolic rate. Recalculate every four to six weeks to keep your targets accurate.</p>
</section>

<section class="formula">
    <h2>The Mifflin-St Jeor Formula</h2>
    <p>This TDEE calculator uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, which is the formula most registered dietitians recommend today. It was published in 1990 and has been validated in multiple clinical studies since then.</p>
    <p><strong>Step 1, Calculate BMR:</strong></p>
    <p>Men: BMR = (10 x weight kg) + (6.25 x height cm) - (5 x age) + 5</p>
    <p>Women: BMR = (10 x weight kg) + (6.25 x height cm) - (5 x age) - 161</p>
    <p><strong>Step 2, Multiply by your activity factor:</strong></p>
    <ul>
        <li>Sedentary: BMR x 1.2</li>
        <li>Lightly active: BMR x 1.375</li>
        <li>Moderately active: BMR x 1.55</li>
        <li>Very active: BMR x 1.725</li>
        <li>Extra active: BMR x 1.9</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The result is your TDEE, the actual number of calories you burn in a day accounting for your lifestyle.</p>
</section>

