<section class="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>Why does activity level change my TDEE so much?</h3>
    <p>The activity multiplier is the single biggest variable in the formula. The difference between sedentary and very active is a multiplier of 1.2 vs 1.725. For a person with a BMR of 1,800 that means a gap of nearly 950 calories per day. Choosing the wrong level throws off your whole plan.</p>

    <h3>How often should I recalculate my TDEE?</h3>
    <p>Every four to six weeks is a reasonable schedule, or any time your weight changes by more than three kilograms. As you lose fat your BMR decreases slightly, so the deficit that worked before slowly becomes less effective without adjustment.</p>

    <h3>What is the difference between BMR and TDEE?</h3>
    <p>BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) is how many calories your body burns at complete rest, basically just to keep your organs running. TDEE adds your daily movement and exercise on top. Eating at your TDEE holds your weight steady. Eating below it causes fat loss; above it causes weight gain.</p>

    <h3>Is this calculator better than other TDEE calculators?</h3>
    <p>All calculators using Mifflin-St Jeor will produce similar numbers because the formula is the same. The bigger factor is how accurately you enter your data and how honestly you select your activity level. No online tool can measure actual calorie burn; it can only produce an evidence-based estimate.</p>
</section>

